Windows doesn't _come with_ pretty much anything.  It's
basically just a minimal base system (albeit with a GUI),
not a full distro.  The usual procedure for Windows users,
when they want to actually _use_ their computer for any
purpose, is to separately obtain and install third-party
software for that purpose.  Editors are no exception, and
all the major ones are available for Windows, up to and
including Emacs.  There are also numerous Windows-only
editors, some of which are halfway decent, but there's
no need to concern yourself with them if you're already
comfortable with some particular editor found on other
systems; you can just get (the Windows version of) that
and use it.

With that said, getting Unix-oriented things to build on
Windows can be a real pain.  A lot of the usual build tools
(like automake and autoconf) mostly don't work on Windows,
and libraries that you want to take for granted have to
be specially obtained and built, and then if you want to
distribute a binary you have to figure out how to package
the built libraries with it...

Major applications (like Perl and Emacs and OO.o and
Firefox and so on and so forth) always have well-documented
build procedures for Windows that work reliably, and in any
case they are also consistently available in prebuilt form
wrapped up in a nice installer, but this is because people
have put substantial effort into making it so.

> Windows not coming with a decent editor for code, but
> hopefully I'll be able to obtain one at some point

As far as the editor, you can just do a web search for
e.g. "Emacs for Windows" (or replace the word Emacs with the
name of whichever editor you prefer).  The Windows version
of Emacs is if anything very slightly _better_ than X11
versions, because the Emacs concept of a kill ring with a
single item on top fits better with the way Windows does
copy and paste than it does with X's oddball dichotomy
between selection buffer and cut buffer.



